              id: 913, category: "Final Year (Part 2)", subject: "Medicine", theme: "amber",
              title: "Management of Acute Severe Asthma & COPD Exacerbation", 
              preview: "Nebulized bronchodilators, systemic steroids, IV magnesium sulfate, and NIV criteria...", 
              body: "<p><b>Acute Severe Asthma / COPD Exacerbation Management:</b><br>• <b>1. Oxygen Therapy:</b> Target saturation 94–98% for asthma; 88–92% for COPD (to prevent suppression of hypoxic respiratory drive leading to CO2 narcosis).<br>• <b>2. Bronchodilators:</b> Frequent or continuous nebulization of short-acting beta-2 agonists (Salbutamol) combined with short-acting muscarinic antagonists (Ipratropium bromide).<br>• <b>3. Systemic Corticosteroids:</b> IV Hydrocortisone (100 mg every 6h) or Oral Prednisolone (40-50 mg daily) must be given early (takes 4-6 hours to kick in).<br>• <b>4. Adjunctive Therapies:</b> IV Magnesium Sulfate (2g over 20 min) in severe acute asthma refractory to initial treatment.<br>• <b>5. Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV / BiPAP):</b> Indicated in acute COPD exacerbations with persistent respiratory acidosis (pH &lt; 7.35 and pCO2 &gt; 45 mmHg). Endotracheal intubation is the final resort if NIV fails.</p>" 
