              body: "<p><b>Immediate Resuscitation & Stabilization:</b><br>• Two large-bore IV cannulas, aggressive fluid resuscitation, and blood transfusion (maintain Hb &gt; 7 g/dL, or &gt; 9 g/dL in coronary artery disease).<br>• Correct coagulopathy with Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP) or platelets if INR &gt; 1.5 or platelets &lt; 50,000.<br>• <b>High-dose PPI Infusion:</b> IV Pantoprazole bolus followed by continuous infusion (8 mg/hr) for non-variceal bleeds.</p><p><b>Variceal Bleeding Specifics:</b><br>• If variceal bleed is suspected (e.g., chronic liver disease / ascites): Start <b>Vasoactive Drugs</b> (Terlipressin, Octreotide, or Somatostatin) immediately to lower portal pressure.<br>• Administer prophylactic antibiotics (Ceftriaxone or Norfloxacin) to prevent spontaneous bacterial peritonitis / sepsis.<br>• Perform diagnostic and therapeutic <b>Upper GI Endoscopy (UGIE) within 12 hours</b> for variceal band ligation (VBL) or sclerotherapy.</p>" 
