              body: "<p><b>1. Epidural Hematoma (EDH):</b><br>• <i>Vessel Ruptured:</i> <b>Middle Meningeal Artery</b> (often associated with temporal bone fracture).<br>• <i>Clinical Hallmarks:</i> Classic <b>'Lucid Interval'</b> where the patient is initially conscious after trauma, followed by rapid neurological deterioration as the hematoma expands.<br>• <b>CT Head Appearance:</b> Biconvex, <b>Lens-shaped hyperdensity</b> that is sharply bounded by cranial suture lines (because periosteum is tightly adherent to sutures).</p><p><b>2. Subdural Hematoma (SDH):</b><br>• <i>Vessel Ruptured:</i> <b>Bridging Veins</b> (tears due to deceleration or rotational shear forces; common in elderly and alcoholics with cortical atrophy).<br>• <b>CT Head Appearance:</b> Crescent-shaped, <b>Concave hyperdensity</b> (or hypodense/isodense if chronic) that <b>can cross cranial suture lines</b>.<br>• <i>Associated Sign:</i> Often accompanied by extensive underlying parenchymal contusion and a significant <b>Midline Shift</b> across the falx cerebri, causing uncal herniation and compression of CN III (blown pupil).</p>" 
